<Base title="How App Composer works" description="Understand the supported agent-operated workflow, independent app contracts, ownership, configuration, updates and safe rollback."><h1>How App Composer works</h1><p class="lede">App Composer prepares a real environment and joins independently maintained apps. It uses external Codex for execution and EmDash for its managed catalogue; it does not host a competing agent.</p><p><a href="/architecture">Read the original visual architecture explainers</a></p><section class="section"><h2>From no idea to a live app</h2><ol><li><strong>Get Ready.</strong> The agent checks its actual workspace, GitHub and Cloudflare tools. It verifies the target and deploys the configurable Hello World reference over HTTPS.</li><li><strong>Init.</strong> Choose a useful preset and customise real fields. Export a versioned spec and a matching prompt, without credentials.</li><li><strong>Compose.</strong> Pin independent source commits, validate their declared routes, data versions and capabilities, and connect their real item/action interface.</li><li><strong>Verify.</strong> Check source identity and live interaction, not just HTTP 200 or a green build.</li></ol></section><section class="section" id="standalone-get-ready"><h2>Get Ready works on its own.</h2><p>The same reusable core powers Composer’s readiness command and an independent Node consumer. It checks the actual agent, GitHub access, Cloudflare account and HTTPS source, saves private progress and rechecks it on resume. A provider interruption stays with the agent for retry; confirmed authentication or permission problems identify the human step.</p><p>The independent consumer has redeployed the reviewed Hello reference and verified its exact source on the existing custom domain. Deployment is an explicit operation with fresh prerequisite, source, account and route checks. It cannot run an arbitrary repository or create infrastructure.</p><p><a href="https://github.com/loftwah/app-composer/tree/main/lib/get-ready">Read the MIT core</a> · <a href="https://github.com/loftwah/app-composer/blob/main/examples/get-ready.mjs">Use the standalone example</a></p><p class="note">The supported Mac path can install missing pinned dependencies in a clean, reviewed Hello checkout, verify its local Wrangler and resume deployment. A repeat leaves working tools alone. Global tool installation, initial account/domain setup and fresh-user provisioning remain work to prove. The local programmatic boundary is available; a hosted API and Get Ready MCP server are not exposed yet. Composer and the dashboard extension currently hand execution to your authorised agent.</p></section><section class="section"><h2>The first composition</h2><p>Photo Guestbook owns submitted media, moderation and eligibility. Curated Showcase owns its card collection. An approved item can be selected and featured; opening its card opens the source contribution. Unavailable, hidden or deleted sources fail closed. Composer joins the routes through Worker service bindings; there are no iframes or private media copies.</p><p><a href="/catalogue">Browse the EmDash catalogue</a> · <a href="/demo">Use the live pair</a></p></section><section class="section"><h2>Updates preserve your work.</h2><p>App configuration and owner data live separately from upstream source. A candidate must keep the supported contract and data version, pass capability checks and use exact commits. Incompatible versions or new permissions are rejected before activation. Your working release stays live.</p><p>The first path uses explicit, validated promotion through repository commands. Automatic branch following and webhook-driven updates remain unshipped until their concurrency and conflict gates are proven.</p><p>Code rollback restores an executable only. It never restores an old database, republishes a hidden image or discards later submissions. Storage exports and backups have their own authorised recovery path.</p></section><section class="section"><h2>The supported boundary</h2><p>This first release is operated by an authorised agent using its own GitHub/Cloudflare runtime. Public users can browse examples, explore decisions and export a spec. They cannot launch arbitrary builds, access production credentials or moderate another owner’s collection.</p><p>Owner uploads are capped and invited. No current feature implies a public multi-tenant security guarantee, provider partnership or commercial agreement.</p><p><a href="/state">Try fictional encrypted assistant state</a> · <a href="/extension">Optional Cloudflare dashboard extension</a> · <a href="/release">Verified release evidence</a></p></section></Base>
